#: ../../../maintenance.md:1
msgid "Maintainers Guide"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:3
msgid ""
"This page contains instructions for project maintainers about how our setup "
"works, making releases, creating packages, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:6
msgid ""
"If you want to make a contribution to the project, see the [](contributing."
"md) instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:8
msgid "Onboarding/Offboarding Access Checklist"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:10
msgid ""
"Note that anyone can contribute to PyGMT, even without being added to the "
"[GenericMappingTools team](https://github.com/orgs/GenericMappingTools/"
"teams). The onboarding items below are for people who would like to make "
"regular contributions, and could benefit from extra permissions to the "
"developer and communication tools we use."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:16
msgid "As a Contributor"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:18
msgid ""
"Add to the [pygmt-contributors team](https://github.com/orgs/"
"GenericMappingTools/teams/pygmt-contributors) (gives 'write' permission to "
"the repository)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:19
msgid ""
"Add as a member on [HackMD](https://hackmd.io/@pygmt) (for draft "
"announcements) [optional]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:21
msgid "As a Maintainer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:23
msgid ""
"Add to the [pygmt-maintainers team](https://github.com/orgs/"
"GenericMappingTools/teams/pygmt-maintainers) (gives 'maintain' permission to "
"the repository)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:24
msgid "Add to \"Active Maintainers\" on the [Team Gallery page](team.md)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:25
msgid ""
"Add as a moderator on the [GMT forum](https://forum.generic-mapping-tools."
"org) (to see mod-only discussions) [optional]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:26
msgid ""
"Add as a maintainer on [ReadtheDocs](https://readthedocs.org/projects/pygmt-"
"dev) [optional]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:27
msgid ""
"Add as a curator to the [GMT community](https://zenodo.org/communities/"
"generic-mapping-tools/) on Zenodo (for making releases) [optional]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:29
msgid "As an Administrator"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:31
msgid ""
"Add to the [pygmt-admin team](https://github.com/orgs/GenericMappingTools/"
"teams/pygmt-admin) (gives 'admin' permission to the repository)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:32
msgid ""
"Add as a maintainer on [PyPI](https://pypi.org/project/pygmt/) and [Test "
"PyPI](https://test.pypi.org/project/pygmt) [optional]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:34
msgid ""
"**Note**: When a maintainer is no longer active (no activity in one year), "
"we will mirror the onboarding access checklist:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:37
msgid ""
"Move from the [pygmt-maintainers team](https://github.com/orgs/"
"GenericMappingTools/teams/pygmt-maintainers) to the [pygmt-contributors team]"
"(https://github.com/orgs/GenericMappingTools/teams/pygmt-contributors)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:39
msgid ""
"Move from \"Active Maintainers\" to \"Distinguished Contributors\" on the "
"[Team Gallery page](team.md)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:40
msgid "Remove 'maintain' permission from GMT forum, ReadTheDocs, Zenodo"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:42
msgid "Branches"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:44
msgid ""
"*main*: Always tested and ready to become a new version. Don't push directly "
"to this branch. Make a new branch and submit a pull request instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:46
msgid ""
"*gh-pages*: Holds the HTML documentation and is served by GitHub. Pages for "
"the main branch are in the `dev` folder. Pages for each release are in their "
"own folders. **Automatically updated by GitHub Actions** so you shouldn't "
"have to make commits here."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:50
msgid "Managing GitHub Issues"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:52
msgid "A few guidelines for managing GitHub issues:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:54
msgid ""
"Assign [labels](https://github.com/GenericMappingTools/pygmt/labels) and the "
"expected [milestone](https://github.com/GenericMappingTools/pygmt/"
"milestones) to issues as appropriate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:57
msgid ""
"When people request to work on an open issue, either assign the issue to "
"that person and post a comment about the assignment or explain why you are "
"not assigning the issue to them and, if possible, recommend other issues for "
"them to work on."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:60
msgid ""
"People with write access should self-assign issues and/or comment on the "
"issues that they will address."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:62
msgid ""
"For upstream bugs, close the issue after an upstream release fixes the bug. "
"If possible, post a comment when an upstream PR is merged that fixes the "
"problem, and consider adding a regression test for serious bugs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:66
msgid "Reviewing and Merging Pull Requests"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:68
msgid "A few guidelines for reviewing:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:70
msgid "Always **be polite** and give constructive feedback."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:71
msgid ""
"Welcome new users and thank them for their time, even if we don't plan on "
"merging the PR."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:73
msgid ""
"Don't be harsh with code style or performance. If the code is bad, either "
"(1) merge the pull request and open a new one fixing the code and pinging "
"the original submitter or (2) comment on the PR detailing how the code could "
"be improved. Both ways are focused on showing the contributor **how to write "
"good code**, not shaming them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:78
msgid ""
"Pull requests should be **squash merged**. This means that all commits will "
"be collapsed into one. The main advantages of this are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:82
msgid "Eliminates experimental commits or commits to undo previous changes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:83
msgid ""
"Makes sure every commit on the main branch passes the tests and has a "
"defined purpose."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:84
msgid ""
"The maintainer writes the final commit message, so we can make sure it's "
"good and descriptive."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:88
msgid "Continuous Integration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:90
msgid ""
"We use GitHub Actions continuous integration (CI) services to build, test "
"and manage the project on Linux, macOS and Windows. The GitHub Actions CI "
"are controlled by workflow files located in `.github/workflows`. Here we "
"briefly summarize the functions of the workflows. Please refer to the "
"comments in the workflow files for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:96
msgid ""
"`benchmarks.yml`: Benchmark the execution speed of tests to track "
"performance of PyGMT functions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:97
msgid "`cache_data.yaml`: Cache GMT remote data files and upload as artifacts"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:98
msgid "`check-links.yml`: Check links in the repository and documentation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:99
msgid ""
"`ci_docs.yml`: Build documentation on Linux/macOS/Windows and deploy to "
"GitHub"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:100
msgid "`ci_doctest.yaml`: Run all doctests on Linux/macOS/Windows"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:101
msgid "`ci_tests.yaml`: Run regular PyGMT tests on Linux/macOS/Windows"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:102
msgid ""
"`ci_tests_dev.yaml`: Run regular PyGMT tests with GMT dev version on Linux/"
"macOS/Windows"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:103
msgid ""
"`ci_tests_legacy.yaml`: Run regular PyGMT tests with GMT legacy versions on "
"Linux/macOS/Windows"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:104
msgid "`format-command.yml`: Format the codes using slash command"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:105
msgid "`publish-to-pypi.yml`: Publish archives to PyPI and TestPyPI"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:106
msgid ""
"`release-baseline-images.yml`: Upload the ZIP archive of baseline images as "
"a release asset"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:107
msgid "`release-drafter.yml`: Draft the next release notes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:108
msgid "`slash-command-dispatch.yml`: Support slash commands in pull requests"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:109
msgid "`style_checks.yaml`: Code lint and style checks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:110
msgid "`type_checks.yml`: Static type checks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:113
msgid "Continuous Documentation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:115
msgid ""
"We use the [ReadtheDocs](https://readthedocs.org/) service to preview "
"changes made to our documentation website every time we make a commit in a "
"pull request. The service has a configuration file `.readthedocs.yaml`, with "
"a list of options to change the default behaviour at <https://docs."
"readthedocs.io/en/stable/config-file/index.html>."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:120
msgid "Continuous Benchmarking"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:122
msgid ""
"We use the [CodSpeed](https://codspeed.io) service to continuously track "
"PyGMT's performance. The `pytest-codspeed` plugin collects benchmark data "
"and uploads it to the CodSpeed server, where results are available at "
"<https://codspeed.io/GenericMappingTools/pygmt>."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:126
msgid ""
"Benchmarking is handled through the `benchmarks.yml` GitHub Actions "
"workflow. It's automatically executed when a pull request is merged into the "
"main branch. To trigger benchmarking in a pull request, add the `run/"
"benchmark` label to the pull request."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:130
msgid ""
"To include a new test in the benchmark suite, apply the `@pytest.mark."
"benchmark` decorator to a test function."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:133
msgid "Dependencies Policy"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:135
msgid ""
"PyGMT has adopted [SPEC 0](https://scientific-python.org/specs/spec-0000/) "
"alongside the rest of the scientific Python ecosystem, and made a few "
"extensions based on the needs of the project. Please see [Minimum Supported "
"Versions](minversions.md) for the detailed policy and the minimum supported "
"versions of GMT, Python and core package dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:140
msgid ""
"In `pyproject.toml`, the `requires-python` key should be set to the minimum "
"supported version of Python. Minimum supported versions of GMT, Python and "
"core package dependencies should be adjusted upward on every major and minor "
"release, but never on a patch release."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:146
msgid "Backwards Compatibility and Deprecation Policy"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:148
msgid ""
"PyGMT is still undergoing rapid development. All of the API is subject to "
"change until the 1.0.0 release. Versioning in PyGMT is based on the "
"[semantic versioning specification](https://semver.org/spec/v2.0.0.html) (i."
"e., *MAJOR*.*MINOR*.*PATCH*). Basic policy for backwards compatibility:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:153
msgid ""
"Any incompatible changes should go through the deprecation process below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:154
msgid ""
"Incompatible changes are only allowed in major and minor releases, not in "
"patch releases."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:155
msgid "Incompatible changes should be documented in the release notes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:157
msgid "When making incompatible changes, we should follow the process:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:159
msgid "Discuss whether the incompatible changes are necessary on GitHub."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:160
msgid ""
"Make the changes in a backwards compatible way, and raise a `FutureWarning` "
"warning for the old usage. At least one test using the old usage should be "
"added."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:162
msgid ""
"The warning message should clearly explain the changes and include the "
"versions in which the old usage is deprecated and is expected to be removed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:164
msgid ""
"The `FutureWarning` warning should appear for 2-4 minor versions, depending "
"on the impact of the changes. It means the deprecation period usually lasts "
"3-12 months."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:166
msgid "Remove the old usage and warning when reaching the declared version."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:168
msgid "Deprecating a function parameter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:170
msgid ""
"To rename a function parameter, add the `@deprecate_parameter` decorator "
"near the top after the `@fmt_docstring` decorator but before the "
"`@use_alias` decorator (if those two exist). A `TODO` comment should also be "
"added to indicate the deprecation period (see below). Here is an example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:175
msgid ""
"# TODO(PyGMT>=0.6.0): Remove the deprecated \"columns\" parameter.\n"
"@fmt_docstring\n"
"@deprecate_parameter(\"columns\", \"incols\", \"0.4.0\", "
"remove_version=\"0.6.0\")\n"
"@use_alias(J=\"projection\", R=\"region\", V=\"verbose\", i=\"incols\")\n"
"@kwargs_to_strings(R=\"sequence\", i=\"sequence_comma\")\n"
"def plot(self, x=None, y=None, data=None, size=None, direction=None, "
"**kwargs):\n"
"    pass\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:185
msgid ""
"In this case, the old parameter name `columns` is deprecated since 0.4.0, "
"and will be fully removed in 0.6.0. The new parameter name is `incols`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:188
msgid "TODO comments"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:190
msgid ""
"Occasionally, we need to implement temporary code that should be removed in "
"the future. This can occur in situations such as:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:193
msgid ""
"When a parameter, function, or method is deprecated and scheduled for "
"removal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:194
msgid ""
"When workarounds are necessary to address issues in older or upcoming "
"versions of GMT or other dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:197
msgid ""
"To track these temporary codes or workarounds, we use TODO comments. These "
"comments should adhere to the following format:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:200
msgid ""
"# TODO(package>=X.Y.Z): A brief description of the TODO item.\n"
"# Additional details if necessary.\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:204
msgid ""
"The TODO comment indicates that we should address the item when *package* "
"version *X.Y.Z* or later is required."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:207
msgid ""
"It's important not to overuse TODO comments for tracking unimplemented "
"features. Instead, open issues to monitor these features."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:211
msgid "Making a Release"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:213
msgid ""
"We try to automate the release process as much as possible. GitHub Actions "
"workflow handles publishing new releases to PyPI and updating the "
"documentation. The version number is set automatically using [setuptools_scm]"
"(https://pypi.org/project/setuptools-scm/) based information obtained from "
"git. There are a few steps that still must be done manually, though."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:219
msgid "Updating the Changelog"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:221
msgid ""
"The [Release Drafter](https://github.com/release-drafter/release-drafter) "
"GitHub Action will automatically keep a draft changelog at <https://github."
"com/GenericMappingTools/pygmt/releases>, adding a new entry every time a "
"pull request (with a proper label) is merged into the main branch. This "
"release drafter tool has two configuration files, one for the GitHub Action "
"at `.github/workflows/release-drafter.yml`, and one for the changelog "
"template at `.github/release-drafter.yml`. Configuration settings can be "
"found at <https://github.com/release-drafter/release-drafter>."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:230
msgid ""
"The drafted release notes are not perfect, so we will need to tidy it prior "
"to publishing the actual release notes at [](changes.md)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:233
msgid ""
"Go to <https://github.com/GenericMappingTools/pygmt/releases> and click on "
"the 'Edit' button next to the current draft release note. Copy the text of "
"the automatically drafted release notes under the 'Write' tab to `doc/"
"changes.md`. Add a section separator `---` between the new and old changelog "
"sections."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:238
msgid ""
"Update the DOI badge in the changelog. Remember to replace the DOI number "
"inside the badge url."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:241
msgid ""
"[![Digital Object Identifier for PyGMT vX.Y.Z](https://img.shields.io/badge/"
"DOI-10.5281/zenodo.<INSERT-DOI-HERE>-blue)](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo."
"<INSERT-DOI-HERE>)\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:244
msgid ""
"Open a new pull request using the title 'Changelog entry for vX.Y.Z' with "
"the updated release notes, so that other people can help to review and "
"collaborate on the changelog curation process described next."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:247
msgid ""
"Edit the change list to remove any trivial changes (updates to the README, "
"typo fixes, CI configuration, test updates due to GMT releases, etc)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:249
msgid ""
"Sort the items within each section (i.e., New Features, Enhancements, etc.) "
"such that similar items are located near each other (e.g., new wrapped "
"modules and methods, gallery examples, API docs changes) and entries within "
"each group are alphabetical."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:253
msgid ""
"Move a few important items from the main sections to the Highlights section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:254
msgid ""
"Edit the list of people who contributed to the release, linking to their "
"GitHub accounts. Sort their names by the number of contributions (including "
"commits and PR reviews) made since the last release (e.g., use `git shortlog "
"vX.Y.Z..HEAD -sne --group=author --group=trailer:co-authored-by`). Keep the "
"contributors as a comma-separated list (one name per line with a trailing "
"comma), rather than a bulleted list, to save vertical space."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:260
msgid ""
"Update `doc/minversions.md` with new information on the new release version, "
"including a vX.Y.Z documentation link, and minimum required versions of GMT, "
"Python and core package dependencies (NumPy, pandas, xarray). Follow [SPEC 0]"
"(https://scientific-python.org/specs/spec-0000/) for updates."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:264
msgid ""
"Refresh citation information. Specifically, the BibTeX in `README.md` and "
"`CITATION.cff` needs to be updated with any metadata changes, including the "
"DOI, release date, and version information. Please also follow guidelines in "
"`AUTHORSHIP.md` for updating the author list in the BibTeX. More information "
"about the `CITATION.cff` specification can be found at <https://github.com/"
"citation-file-format/citation-file-format/blob/main/schema-guide.md>."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:271
msgid "Pushing to PyPI and Updating the Documentation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:273
msgid ""
"After the changelog is updated, making a release can be done by going to "
"<https://github.com/GenericMappingTools/pygmt/releases>, editing the draft "
"release, and clicking on publish. A git tag will also be created, make sure "
"that this tag is a proper version number (following [Semantic Versioning]"
"(https://semver.org/)) with a leading `v` (e.g., `v0.2.1`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:279
msgid ""
"Once the release/tag is created, this should trigger GitHub Actions to do "
"all the work for us. A new source distribution will be uploaded to PyPI, a "
"new folder with the documentation HTML will be pushed to *gh-pages*, and the "
"`latest` link will be updated to point to this new folder."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:284
msgid "Archiving on Zenodo"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:286
msgid ""
"Grab both the source code and baseline images ZIP files from the GitHub "
"release page and upload them to Zenodo using the previously reserved DOI."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:289
msgid "Updating the Conda Package"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:291
msgid ""
"When a new version is released on PyPI, conda-forge's bot automatically "
"creates version updates for the feedstock. In most cases, the maintainers "
"can simply merge that PR."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:294
msgid "If changes need to be done manually, you can:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:296
msgid ""
"Fork the [pygmt feedstock repository](https://github.com/conda-forge/pygmt-"
"feedstock) if you haven't already. If you have a fork, update it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:298
msgid ""
"Update the version number and sha256 hash on `recipe/meta.yaml`. You can get "
"the hash from the [PyPI \"Download files\" section](https://pypi.org/project/"
"pygmt/#files)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:300
msgid ""
"Add or remove any new dependencies (most are probably only `run` "
"dependencies)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:301
msgid ""
"Make sure the minimum support versions of all dependencies are correctly "
"pinned."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:302
msgid ""
"Make a new branch, commit, and push the changes **to your personal fork**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:303
msgid "Create a PR against the original feedstock main."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../maintenance.md:304
msgid "Once the CI tests pass, merge the PR or ask a maintainer to do so."
msgstr ""
